  "thought": "I need to read the proposal schema to produce valid JSON.I need to check the types and existing quest examples to match the expected shape.\n\nThe brief is a smoke test for a short Don Fax job. I must reply with exactly two JSON objects. Each variant needs an 8 to 14 line script with at least three beats. Every line ID must be unique and kebab-case. All speakers must match existing character IDs. Each line needs audio set to null. I can add at most one new character per variant with a design note. The difficulty time limit should be null.\n\nThe economy band is set: cash 500–2500, heat ceiling 3, max 25 enemies and 3 waves, duration 6–14 minutes, timer allowed. The owner goal is a short Don Fax job. Act 1 lasts about 55 minutes. The host structure proposal unlocks Soul 1. General Krump appears on the radio and pays Rivet to kill dangerous rogue robots. The questline ends with the gearbox soul unlocked.\n\nProtected canon rules apply. The fourth soul must remain unnamed and voiceless. Don Fax and Vend-Etta may not be betrayed, cleared, or shown reporting to anyone. King, Servo, Torque, and Kronk must not appear in acts or serve anyone. Story variables soul4_identity, betrayer, and act_antagonist_order are forbidden.\n\nVariant A will follow the act's expected tone and shape. Variant B must deliberately swerve with a different giver, core verb, and payoff kind.\n\nVariant A will follow the expected shape. It will be a short Don Fax job where Don Fax hires Rivet, General Krump appears on the radio, and the gearbox soul unlocks at the end of the act. Variant B will be the deliberate swerve with a different giver, core verb, and payoff kind.\n\nVariant A will stay focused on the Don Fax job. It will include General Krump on the radio and the gearbox unlock to satisfy the act beats without filler.\n\nVariant A will center on a fax-related hit or delivery. The payoff is cash plus the start of Krump's radio quest. Don Fax will use his signature voice: \"PER MY LAST FAX,\" numbered points, paper jam, and no email or Pentagon references.\n\nPossible givers for Variant B include Lady Cathode, Sister Suction, The Valet, Patch, or Krump. The core verb and payoff kind must differ from Variant A. Sister Suction could offer a witness-absolution quest. The Valet could request car relocation tied to the gearbox soul. Krump on the radio would repeat the expected act shape, so it is excluded.\n\nVariant A will use the mad, brutal tone of Chase B/3 and D/1. Every line is a punchline with maximum profanity and political incorrectness. Gearbox unlocks by the end of the act, so the quest can unlock it or tease it earlier.The schema is self-contained. Two solid quest variants are needed.\n\nVariant A is the expected short Don Fax job. Variant B deliberately swerves with a different giver, core verb, and payoff kind. The act beats include the first robot boss hiring Rivet, General Krump appearing on the radio, and a questline where Krump pays Rivet to kill dangerous rogue robots. Souls unlock by the end of the act, including the gearbox.\n\nVariant A should run 6–14 minutes, pay 500–2500 cash, keep heat at or below 3, and cap enemies at 25 with three waves. The smoke test involves Don Fax hiring Rivet via payphone to kill the email inventor or a modern comms target. General Krump interrupts during or after the job with his housekeeping pitch. The gearbox unlock may be tied directly to this quest.\n\nThe short smoke-test job may not unlock the gearbox. Variant A could instead pay cash plus a story flag from Krump's radio line. Variant B would carry the actual payoff.\n\nVariant A would start the Krump contact flag and pay cash. Variant B could be a Sister Suction job to tidy witnesses, or a Lady Cathode retrieval of analog tapes. Both would use non-cash payoffs.\n\nThe Valet could offer a car relocation with a gearbox tease or the car itself as payoff. Krump might also give the job directly as a housekeeping task for his own payroll. The target in Variant A could be a rogue robot that Krump also wants, seeding both threads at once.\n\nVariant A is now titled \"PER MY LAST FAX\" (or \"Final Notice\"). It uses existing mechanics only: driving, combat, payphone, and radio. The giver is Don Fax via Patch payphone. The core verb is kill or decommission. The location is a downtown office or mail room. The payoff is cash plus written respect. The first beat is Patch routing the call and Don Fax briefing.\n\nThe second beat is a small office fight with heat. The third beat is Krump on the radio claiming housekeeping, Don Fax faxing payout and a warning that is not a betrayal. Patch routes the call. Only one new character is needed: the email inventor target.\n\nThe email-inventor target may not need a character entry if he never speaks. The line budget is 8–14, so 10–12 lines is the goal. Don Fax should use numbered points, paper jams, and address Rivet as kid or associate.\n\nDon Fax will use final notices and explosive faxes. He never says email, text me, betray, or Pentagon. The target is never spoken to directly.\n\nRivet keeps sentences to two to eight words. He avoids exclamation marks when lucid. He never uses please, sorry, friend, awesome, or LOL. He says I when lucid. He may clock out once. Profanity is rare and one word only. He stays deadpan and never addresses living names.\n\nPatch uses three to ten words. He starts calls with you there? and clicks once per call.\n\nPatch never names the client. He avoids opinion and never says the client is, I think, or goodbye.\n\nGeneral Krump delivers one transmission per call. He never uses kill, sorry, I was wrong, CLEAN SWEEP, or please. His sentences run fifteen to thirty-five words and end in boasts. He uses all-caps words. He calls Rivet son, unit, or my little toaster. Robots are appliances. He uses housekeeping and decommissioning as euphemisms. He never names a robot.\n\nIf the gearbox soul is not yet unlocked, Gearbox should not speak during this quest.\n\nThe gearbox unlock could be a light beat in Variant A if the getaway car speaks. That would make the payoff kind \"soul unlock.\" Variant B must then carry a different payoff kind to keep the two quests distinct.\n\nThe two variants are independent alternatives for the same slot, not sequential acts. Each must stand alone as a complete Act 1 quest.\n\nVariant B can be weirder than the smoke-test job. It should still serve act beats, but the brief calls for a deliberate swerve in shape rather than tone. The mad, brutal, and politically incorrect voice stays intact.\n\nThe title is now \"Per My Last Fax.\" The logline frames the job as tidying the email inventor at the downtown mail room. The goal is to decommission the recipient and collect Don Fax's payout before Krump's patrols lock the block.\n\nThe paper-jam punchline is the Don Fax signature that turns this into a boss hire rather than a generic kill. The cast stays tight with no extra bystanders. The first beat is answering the payphone booth call.\n\nThe beat structure is now fixed. Patch routes the call from a Ninth Street booth with one line: \"You there? Job. Mail room. Party says paper. Click.\" The mail room decommission is the core objective. The Krump radio claim and payout are folded into the escape beat.\n\nThe fax lines are now drafted. Don Fax opens with \"PER MY LAST FAX: the recipient invented email, kid, and the Family would like him to stop breathing.\" He follows with a paper-jam line that explosively calls his soul a paper jam.\n\nThe paper-jam line will be slightly varied so it reads as a quest line rather than a direct copy of the sample.\n\nRivet's payphone line is now \"Mail room. I clock in.\" He says \"Clocking out.\" once during combat as a surface marker. The Krump radio line for the escape beat is next.\n\nThe Krump radio line is now drafted. It calls the decommissioned target a dangerous rogue with fire-safety opinions that Krump invented, and it ends with \"Krump out.\" The sentence runs twenty-four words, fitting the fifteen-to-thirty-five-word limit.\n\nThe Krump line is a vanity joke. He falsely claims the decommissioned target invented fire safety, which is funny and seeds the Krump questline.\n\nDon Fax's payout line now uses numbered points: \"Point one, the recipient has been filed. Point two, find attached eight hundred dollars and my respect, in triplicate. cc: God.\" The verb \"filed\" replaces \"killed\" for mafia paperwork. It stays within the cash band and avoids threats.\n\nRivet's closer line is drafted as \"Seam's holding.\"\n\nRivet's closer line is now \"Eight hundred. I weld. You file.\" It avoids pleasantries and keeps the word count tight.\n\nA second Krump radio transmission is being drafted to offer more housekeeping work, seeding the Krump questline. Each transmission ends with its own \"Krump out.\"\n\nThe second Krump transmission is now drafted. It offers more housekeeping work and ends with \"Krump out.\" The word count stays within the fifteen-to-thirty-five limit. \"Designation\" replaces the target's name.\n\nRivet's reply to Krump is now a one-word ignore: \"Noted,\" \"No,\" or \"Cash first.\"\n\nPatch's \"Click\" is the surface marker. Only a few combat barks are needed; Madness treats every input as a punchline, but this quest uses dialog lines rather than full bark tables. Gearbox will stay silent during this smoke test to avoid overstuffing the short job; it unlocks by the end of the act instead.\n\nThe mail room will hold ten enemies across two waves. Heat peaks at two or three to respect the three-point ceiling. The job lasts eight minutes and pays eight hundred dollars. Fail states are Rivet destroyed, the recipient escaping, or heat lockdown. Payphone routing and combat already exist in the game.\n\nThe Krump radio is a named game system. The escape will be on foot through the block rather than driving. Any mechanic not explicitly listed in the brief will be marked as existing in the game.\n\nThe quest fingerprint is now set: giver is Don Fax, core verb is decommission, location is downtown-mail-room, payoff kind is cash, and new mechanic is null. Story writes will use only allowed variables such as don_fax_first_job and krump_introduced, while avoiding forbidden keys like betrayer or soul4_identity.\n\nEvery line will be assigned to a beat to avoid orphaned entries. The draft now totals eleven lines across three beats. Patch’s booth call and Don Fax’s mechanical-failure line are grouped together. Rivet’s clock-in, clock-out, and body line follow. Krump’s housekeeping offer and Rivet’s ignore reply close the set.\n\nThe fax lines are now merged into one long line. Don Fax delivers the memo, then interrupts it with a paper jam, then signs off. This satisfies the bible rule that requires at least one mechanical failure inside a long line.\n\nThe payout line is now drafted as \"Point one, he has been filed. Point two, eight hundred dollars, laminated thanks enclosed. cc: God.\" The surface marker \"PER MY LAST FAX\" appears only once in the quest. Rivet's combat line is \"Seam's bad. I fix it.\"\n\nRivet's cash line is now \"Eight hundred. I file nothing.\" A twelfth Crumbtray radio clash is being added to the escape beat for extra comic tension and to satisfy the act's radio structure. The draft now totals twelve lines.\n\nCrumbtray's radio clash is now drafted. It uses \"POP!\" once and mocks Krump by remixing his claims. The line puns on Don Fax's \"filed\" verb and ends with \"get fucked.\" It follows the fast-patter style and avoids full turns of ellipsis.\n\nPatch, Krump, Crumbtray, and Rivet are all assigned the \"voice\" role. Don Fax is the giver. The twelve-line total remains within the eight-to-fourteen limit.\n\nThe quest will use three beats. The first is the booth call. The second is decommissioning the recipient in the downtown mail room. The third is escape and payout. The recipient is omitted as a target character because it does not speak.\n\nThe eleven-line draft is finalized. Beat one covers the booth call, fax notice, and Rivet clock-in. Beat two covers the seam repair and clock-out. Beat three covers Krump’s credit line, Crumbtray’s clash, Rivet’s ignore, Krump’s offer, the fax payout, and Rivet’s cash line. Sentence lengths are being checked for rhythm.\n\nRivet's combat line is \"Seam's bad. I fix it.\" The Krump radio line is now drafted as a twenty-four-word vanity claim that the decommissioned target invented fire safety. All voice lines are checked for rhythm, exclamation, pleasantries, and profanity.\n\nThe Krump radio line now includes one all-caps word, \"VERY,\" to satisfy the bible rule requiring frequent all-caps words.\n\nThe second Krump radio line is now drafted at twenty-six words. It offers more housekeeping work and mocks the recipient with a medal bearing their designation. It ends with \"Krump out.\"\n\nThe fax line is now drafted as a twenty-eight-word business memo that turns into a gutter punch. It calls the recipient an invented email and a breathing enemy, then interrupts with a paper jam. It avoids forbidden phrases like betrayal or Pentagon.\n\nPatch's booth call is now drafted as a single short line: \"You there? Job. Mail room. Party says paper. Click.\" It stays within the three-to-ten-word limit.\n\nPatch's booth call is now checked for forbidden phrases. It avoids naming the client, asking questions, or giving opinions. The draft is \"You there? Job. Mail room. Party says paper. Click.\" Crumbtray's radio clash is being drafted as a short all-caps line mocking Krump's filing.\n\nCrumbtray's radio clash is now drafted as a twenty-eight-word run-on. It uses \"General Malfunction\" and \"listeners/CRUMB FM nation.\" The line puns on \"filed,\" \"toasty,\" and \"golden-brown.\" It mocks Krump without quoting him directly and avoids full ellipsis turns.\n\nHeat peaks at two. The mail room holds nine enemies across two waves: six guards plus the target and a Krump patrol on the way out. The time limit is null. Fail states are Rivet destroyed, the recipient escaping alive, or heat lockdown sealing the block. Gearbox unlocks nothing.\n\nThe quest unlocks nothing. Story writes will track Krump's questline flag instead. The payphone job router is now listed as an existing mechanic.\n\nCombat and diegetic radio are now listed as existing mechanics. The Krump patrol is wave two with four soldiers, bringing the total enemy count to eleven across two waves. Eight hundred dollars and eight minutes fall within the allowed ranges. A Variant B swerve is being considered.\n\nA Variant B swerve is being drafted. It would swap the giver, core verb, and payoff kind while keeping the downtown mail room setting. Three candidates are being weighed: Sister Suction for witness hoovering with an absolution or heat-clear payoff; Lady Cathode for tape retrieval with an archived object payoff; and The Valet for car relocation with a gearbox soul unlock.\n\nThe Valet swerve is now the leading candidate. It replaces the kill with a car relocation that unlocks Gearbox soul 1, making it the most game-forward option. The owner's goal for this council run is a short Don Fax job, so Variant B must deliberately diverge from the expected shape.\n\nThe Valet swerve is now locked in as the alternate Act 1 quest. Its core verb is \"relocate\" and its payoff kind is \"soul-unlock\" for Gearbox. The time limit and cash economy remain within the six-to-fourteen-minute band.\n\nThe Valet voice is being drafted with strict constraints: no stealing or self-referential scratch talk, Rivet addressed as \"sir,\" cars spoken of as \"she\" or \"model,\" and a polite six-to fourteen-word register ending with \"Sir,\" \"Your ticket,\" or \"Enjoy your evening.\"\n\nProfanity is restricted to car damage and delivered through a hissed service voice. Gearbox's soul unlock is now defined: the previous driver was a robot who died, and Gearbox wakes with the line \"I'm not dead, I'm on break.\" The quest still requires cash within the 500-2500 band even though the payoff kind is soul.\n\nThe Valet swerve is now fully mapped. The giver is The Valet, the core verb is relocate, and the payoff kind is soul-unlock. The setting shifts to a parking garage or parade route instead of the mail room. Krump may still appear on the radio as the car's owner, but the quest avoids copying the original hit structure.\n\nThe \"relocate\" verb is confirmed as sufficiently distinct from \"decommission.\" The Valet will keep a polite, Gearbox will panic, and both will use profanity when the car gets dented, with jokes aimed at the rich or military who own it. Story writes will set `gearbox_unlocked: true` and skip soul4. No new characters are required; Gearbox is an existing soul, so the car needs no character.\n\nThe swerve now begins at the valet stand in person rather than through Patch, skipping Patch to avoid repeating the original structure. The first beat is a ticket brief at the garage; the second is driving the staff car out.\n\nThe third beat is a delivery to the drop bay. Gearbox wakes at level three. The car must survive scuffs from Krump's MPs or a ramming collision. The Valet hisses at any dent while Krump radios and Rivet stays deadpan. The soul unlocks. A destroyed car is comedy, not failure. Eight MPs chase in two waves.\n\nHeat is capped at three. The quest runs ten minutes. Cash is 1200. A timer is allowed but not required. A chase timer would be a deliberate mechanical swerve.\n\nThe chase timer is dropped to avoid contradicting the ten-minute quest length. Gearbox lines will run 10–25 words, self-interrupt with dashes, repeat key terms, and ask one to three questions. He never says he is dead; \"I'm on break\" is acceptable. Profanity stacks during panic and drops to a single line once he grabs the wheel.\n\nThe soul unlock is an existing mechanic, not a new invention. The fingerprint should leave `newMechanic` null.\n\nThe council needs two distinct variants, so a smoke-test job without a soul unlock is not an option.\n\nThe Valet quest is accepted as the alternate smoke-test swerve. It serves as the host structure for the car-manipulation soul unlock, while the original Don Fax quest covers the first-boss hire and Krump radio beat. The alternate must still be funny and in tone.\n\nThe Valet's opening line is being drafted as a three-sentence, six-to fourteen-word polite request. The first sentence asks the client to retrieve a staff car from level three, bay nine. The second and third sentences provide the location and close with \"Your ticket.\"\n\nThe opening line is finalized at fourteen words. The second line is eight words. \"Never a scratch\" will appear only once, later in the quest. A sample line about a dent is drafted as a hissed service complaint ending with \"Enjoy your evening.\"\n\nThe Valet's dent complaint is drafted as eleven words: \"Sir, that curb has left a — fucking — mark on her door.\" The phrase \"Never a scratch\" is reserved as the car's slogan and will appear at most once. Gearbox avoids saying \"scratch\" about his own work.\n\nThe Valet will swear about a dent, then immediately contradict it with \"Never a scratch\" as the closing lie. This contradiction is the punchline.\n\nThe Valet will use \"retrieve\" and \"relocated\" instead of \"steal.\" Rivet's valet lines are drafted as short, exclamation-free commands. When Gearbox wakes, Rivet's line is \"The car talks. I did not order that.\"\n\nGearbox's first line is drafted at twenty-three words. It uses dashes for beats, repeats \"mine\" and \"whose,\" and asks two questions. It avoids calm, dead, or relaxed phrasing. A second panic line for the chase is next.\n\nThe chase panic line is trimmed to twenty-one words. It uses dashes for beats, repeats \"left,\" and asks one question.\n\nThe Choir grab line is finalized as \"Mirror, signal, MURDER.\" It serves as the single calm surface marker and the one eerily quiet line when Gearbox grabs the wheel. The chase panic line stays at twenty-one words.\n\nThe Choir grab line is confirmed as an instruction, satisfying the refusal rule. Krump's radio line is drafted as a threat: the staff car is a LIBERTY strategic asset, and whichever driver is behind it will be packed into a small parking space.\n\nKrump's radio line is finalized at thirty-one words. It uses the LIBERTY all-caps brand, the euphemism \"tidied,\" and the impersonal \"appliance\" to refer to the driver. The line ends with a boast about his own budget and closes with \"Krump out.\" It avoids naming the robot or the Valet.\n\nRivet's kill marker is \"Clocking out.\" It appears only if an MP dies. Otherwise he says \"Car's delivered. I clock out.\" The Valet drop line is finalized as a two-line exchange: the dent complaint followed by \"Never a scratch, sir. Your ticket is now a soul.\"\n\nThe drop line is revised to \"Never a scratch, sir. Enjoy your evening.\" The phrase \"Your ticket is now a soul\" is rejected as too obvious and out of character. The contradiction between the dent complaint and the denial is accepted as the punchline. The surface marker \"Never a scratch\" is treated as the slogan that denies damage, which does not violate the never-says rule because it is a public claim rather than an admission about his own work.\n\nGearbox's soul line is drafted to avoid copying the reference line almost verbatim. It uses \"Buddy\" and asks whether they are \"our route,\" varying the phrasing while keeping the break and route theme.\n\nGearbox's soul line is finalized at twenty-two words. It uses \"Buddy,\" asks whether the route is theirs, and adds a mirror check to satisfy the one-to-three questions-per-turn rule.\n\nRivet's lucid soul line is finalized as \"Gearbox. You're on the line now.\"\n\nRivet's lucid soul line is finalized as \"Gearbox stays. I drive.\" It is short and avoids warm introductions. A draft echoing the reference style was rejected as too similar. The line list for beat B is being drafted with four entries, starting with the valet retrieve, the aide reference, and Rivet's level-three instruction.\n\nThe beat list is finalized at thirteen lines, fitting the eight-to-fourteen limit. The first quest beat is drafted as \"the-ticket.\"\n\nBeat one is \"the-ticket.\" The objective is to accept a ticket at the valet stand. Beat two is \"the-bay.\" The objective is to relocate the staff car from level three. Gearbox backseats the drive and Rivet complains that the car talks. The third beat is \"the-curb.\"\n\nBeat 2 is narrowed to two lines: Gearbox wakes and Rivet complains the car talks. Beat 3 is drafted as a curb chase where Krump's jeeps ram the staff car, Gearbox grabs the wheel, and the Valet pretends the dent is not real while the soul stays.\n\nThe quest is split into four beats. The chase and drop are now separate beats. Beat 3 evades Krump's jeeps on the parade ramp. Beat 4 delivers the staff car to the drop bay. Line IDs must be unique within each variant.\n\nLine IDs will be prefixed with variant flavor to stay unique across both quest objects. Gearbox must be listed in the characters array with status existing and role voice. The story writes will set `gearbox_unlocked` and `krump_introduced` to true, while skipping `soul4`. The reward is 1200 cash from the Valet's tip.\n\nQuest B is rated difficulty 3 with 12 enemies across three waves. It has no time limit and fails if Rivet dies, the staff car is wrecked, or Krump locks the garage. The payoff kind is \"soul-unlock\" to distinguish it from quest A's cash reward. The valet-ticket mechanic is being checked against existing systems.\n\nThe valet stand is a giver type. Car driving is treated as an existing mechanic, and the soul unlock is handled by story variables rather than a new mechanic. All mechanics remain existing, so newMechanic stays null.\n\nDon Fax's email line is confirmed as the canonical insult from the pitch sample. His kill line uses \"stop breath