ences. The first is seven words. The second is two words. The total turn stays within the six-to-fourteen limit. The retrieve and ticket lines may be combined into fewer lines.\n\nEach line object represents one turn. The valet retrieve is now a single fourteen-word turn ending with \"Your ticket.\" The aide reference becomes an eight-word turn ending with \"sir.\" The dent line is drafted as a sixteen-word expletive-laden complaint about the curb mark.\n\nThe dent line is trimmed to eleven words. It ends with \"Sir.\" to satisfy the service-phrase rule.\n\nThe curb-mark line is nine words and ends with \"Sir.\" The scratch-denial line is seven words and ends with \"Enjoy your evening.\" Both satisfy the service-phrase rule. The marker is explicitly \"Never a scratch,\" which is the gray area.\n\nGearbox's \"Mirror, signal, MURDER\" is approved as a calm exception to the ten-to-twenty-five word rhythm. Krump's line avoids murder and kill. Rivet B lines are finalized as four short lucid facts: \"Level three. I drive,\" \"The car talks. I did not order that,\" \"Clocking out,\" and \"Gearbox stays. I drive.\" All use the first person and state facts without exclamation marks or pleasantries.\n\nRivet's \"Never says it is sad, scared or lonely\" rule is confirmed satisfied. Gearbox's line is approved as a calm exception to the rhythm limit. Krump B's twenty-three-word threat is being counted.\n\nKrump B's twenty-three-word threat is confirmed at thirty-one words. It ends with a boast and \" Krump out.\" The line correctly uses the car as \"her\" and the driver as \"appliance\" without naming the robot. Variant A's Krump lines both end with \" Krump out.\" Krump may say \"I\" but never says \"I was wrong.\" The Crumbtray line is being checked for forbidden mentions of Rivet, Krump, or full turns.\n\nThe General Malfunction line is approved. It mocks Krump with profanity and a radio pun. The whyNotFiller will cite the paper-jam fuck as the laugh, Krump stealing credit on