her than \"kill\".\n\nThe patch line is nine words and ends with \"Click.\" It avoids questions, client names, and opinions. The fax-notice line is long but only uses \"email\" as an insult. A validator might flag the substring.\n\nThe fax line keeps \"email\" as the Don Fax sample joke. Variant A Rivet lines are five short lucid facts: \"Mail room. I clock in,\" \"Seam's bad. I fix it,\" \"Clocking out,\" \"Not your unit,\" and \"Eight hundred. I file nothing.\" Each is two to eight words, no exclamation marks, no pleasantries.\n\nVariant A beat 3 is ordered as Krump's credit claim, the Crumbtray mock, Rivet's \"Not your unit\" rejection, Krump's offer of more work, the fax payout, and Rivet's \"Eight hundred. I file nothing.\" The one-word replies ignore Krump's status move of never listening.\n\nThe six-turn sequence for beat 3 is now fixed: Krump claims credit, Rivet rejects it, the Crumbtray mocks him, Krump offers a medal anyway, Don Fax pays, and Rivet takes the cash. The Crumbtray line can overlap with Krump's credit claim since Rivet may speak between them. The fax payout will avoid confirming betrayal.\n\nThe fax payout line omits any mention of other recipients except the illustrative \"cc: God\" sign-off. It uses \"laminated thanks\" to match the affection move. The eight-hundred-dollar amount aligns with the reward field. Variant B's twelve-hundred-dollar reward will not be stated in dialogue; the valet only asks for a tip, and the reward field suffices. The Gearbox marker appears exactly once.\n\nThe chase beat objective verb is \"evade.\" The Gearbox wheel grab is treated as a calm play beat inside that evade, not a cutscene. The beat summary will explain the wrestle or give-in.\n\nThe chase beat core verb is \"relocate\" with the wheel grab as one beat. Variant B's twelve enemies are split into three waves of four at the garage exit, street jeeps, and ramp cordon. Heat peaks at three for eleven minutes. JSON will use apostrophes and unicode em dashes